[{"input":"{data-type=\"chapter\"}\n# Parametric and Polar Curves\n\nUntil now, most curves we have encountered have been graphs of functions, and they provided useful visual information about the behaviour of the functions. In this chapter we begin to look at plane curves as interesting objects in their own right.","output":"","result":"{data-type=\"chapter\"}\n# Parametric and Polar Curves\n\nUntil now, most curves we have encountered have been graphs of functions, and they provided useful visual information about the behaviour of the functions. In this chapter we begin to look at plane curves as interesting objects in their own right.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"chapter\" id=\"nb-ES111_071Lecture07-c3893-parametric-and-polar-curves\">\n<h1>Parametric and Polar Curves</h1>\n<p>Until now, most curves we have encountered have been graphs of functions, and they provided useful visual information about the behaviour of the functions. In this chapter we begin to look at plane curves as interesting objects in their own right.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Parametric Curves\n\nSuppose that an object moves around in the ``xy``-plane so that the coordinates of its position at any time ``t`` are continuous functions of the variable ``t``:\n\n```math\nx=f\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y = g\\left(t\\right)\\,.\n```\n\nThe path followed by the object is a curve ``𝒞`` in the plane that is specified by the two equations above. We call these equations *parametric equations* of ``𝒞``. A curve specified by a particular pair of parametric equations is called a *parametric curve*.\n\n::: definition\nA *parametric curve* ``𝒞`` in the plane consists of an ordered pair ``\\left(f, g\\right)`` of continuous functions each defined on the same interval ``I``. The equations\n\n```math\nx=f\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y = g\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{for }t\\in I\\,,\n```\n\nare called *parametric equations* of the curve ``𝒞``. The independent variable ``t`` is called the *parameter*.\n:::\n\nNote that the parametric curve ``𝒞`` was not defined as a set of points in the plane, but rather as the ordered pair of functions whose range is that set of points. Different pairs of functions can give the same set of points in the plane, but we may still want to regard them as different parametric curves.\n\nWe will usually denote the parameter by ``t``; in many applications the parameter represents time, but this need not always be the case. \n\nA parametric curve has a direction (indicated, say, by arrowheads), namely, the direction corresponding to increasing values of the parameter ``t``.\n\n!!! example \"Sketch and identify the parametric curve\"\n\n\t```math\n\tx=t^2-1\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y=t+1\\,,\\quad t\\in \\mathbb{R}\\,.\n\t```\n\n\tWe could construct a table of values of ``x`` and ``y`` for various values of ``t``, thus getting the coordinates of a number of points on a curve. However, for this example it is easier to *eliminate* the parameter from the pair of parametric equations, thus producing a single equation in ``x`` and ``y`` whose graph is the desired curve:\n\n\t```math\n\tt=y - 1\\,,\\quad x=t^2-1=\\left(y-1\\right)^2-1=y^2-2y\\,.\n\t```\n\n\tAll points on the curve lie on the parabola ``x=y^2-2y``. Since ``y\\to\\pm\\infty`` as ``t\\to\\pm\\infty``, the parametric curve is the whole parabola.","output":"","result":"## Parametric Curves\n\nSuppose that an object moves around in the ``xy``-plane so that the coordinates of its position at any time ``t`` are continuous functions of the variable ``t``:\n\n```math\nx=f\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y = g\\left(t\\right)\\,.\n```\n\nThe path followed by the object is a curve ``𝒞`` in the plane that is specified by the two equations above. We call these equations *parametric equations* of ``𝒞``. A curve specified by a particular pair of parametric equations is called a *parametric curve*.\n\n::: definition\nA *parametric curve* ``𝒞`` in the plane consists of an ordered pair ``\\left(f, g\\right)`` of continuous functions each defined on the same interval ``I``. The equations\n\n```math\nx=f\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y = g\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{for }t\\in I\\,,\n```\n\nare called *parametric equations* of the curve ``𝒞``. The independent variable ``t`` is called the *parameter*.\n:::\n\nNote that the parametric curve ``𝒞`` was not defined as a set of points in the plane, but rather as the ordered pair of functions whose range is that set of points. Different pairs of functions can give the same set of points in the plane, but we may still want to regard them as different parametric curves.\n\nWe will usually denote the parameter by ``t``; in many applications the parameter represents time, but this need not always be the case. \n\nA parametric curve has a direction (indicated, say, by arrowheads), namely, the direction corresponding to increasing values of the parameter ``t``.\n\n!!! example \"Sketch and identify the parametric curve\"\n\n\t```math\n\tx=t^2-1\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y=t+1\\,,\\quad t\\in \\mathbb{R}\\,.\n\t```\n\n\tWe could construct a table of values of ``x`` and ``y`` for various values of ``t``, thus getting the coordinates of a number of points on a curve. However, for this example it is easier to *eliminate* the parameter from the pair of parametric equations, thus producing a single equation in ``x`` and ``y`` whose graph is the desired curve:\n\n\t```math\n\tt=y - 1\\,,\\quad x=t^2-1=\\left(y-1\\right)^2-1=y^2-2y\\,.\n\t```\n\n\tAll points on the curve lie on the parabola ``x=y^2-2y``. Since ``y\\to\\pm\\infty`` as ``t\\to\\pm\\infty``, the parametric curve is the whole parabola.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-ES111_071Lecture07-c3895-parametric-curves\">\n<h1>Parametric Curves</h1>\n<p>Suppose that an object moves around in the <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(xy\\)</span>-plane so that the coordinates of its position at any time <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t\\)</span> are continuous functions of the variable <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t\\)</span>:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nx=f\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y = g\\left(t\\right)\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The path followed by the object is a curve <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(𝒞\\)</span> in the plane that is specified by the two equations above. We call these equations <em>parametric equations</em> of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(𝒞\\)</span>. A curve specified by a particular pair of parametric equations is called a <em>parametric curve</em>.</p>\n<div data-type=\"definition\">\n<p>A <em>parametric curve</em> <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(𝒞\\)</span> in the plane consists of an ordered pair <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(f, g\\right)\\)</span> of continuous functions each defined on the same interval <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(I\\)</span>. The equations</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nx=f\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y = g\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{for }t\\in I\\,,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>are called <em>parametric equations</em> of the curve <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(𝒞\\)</span>. The independent variable <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t\\)</span> is called the <em>parameter</em>.</p>\n</div>\n<p>Note that the parametric curve <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(𝒞\\)</span> was not defined as a set of points in the plane, but rather as the ordered pair of functions whose range is that set of points. Different pairs of functions can give the same set of points in the plane, but we may still want to regard them as different parametric curves.</p>\n<p>We will usually denote the parameter by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t\\)</span>; in many applications the parameter represents time, but this need not always be the case.</p>\n<p>A parametric curve has a direction (indicated, say, by arrowheads), namely, the direction corresponding to increasing values of the parameter <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t\\)</span>.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Sketch and identify the parametric curve</h5>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nx=t^2-1\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y=t+1\\,,\\quad t\\in \\mathbb{R}\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>We could construct a table of values of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y\\)</span> for various values of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t\\)</span>, thus getting the coordinates of a number of points on a curve. However, for this example it is easier to <em>eliminate</em> the parameter from the pair of parametric equations, thus producing a single equation in <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y\\)</span> whose graph is the desired curve:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nt=y - 1\\,,\\quad x=t^2-1=\\left(y-1\\right)^2-1=y^2-2y\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>All points on the curve lie on the parabola <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=y^2-2y\\)</span>. Since <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y\\to\\pm\\infty\\)</span> as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t\\to\\pm\\infty\\)</span>, the parametric curve is the whole parabola.</p>\n</aside>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 40;\nconst xmid = 2 * scale;\nconst ymid = 4.5 * scale;\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n  width: 11 * scale,\n  height: 7 * scale,\n  caption: \"The parabola defined parametrically by x = t² − 1, y = t + 1, t ∈ ℝ.\",\n});\n\nfigure.axes({\n  origin: [xmid, ymid],\n  scale,\n});\n\nfigure.label(\"O\", {\n  x: xmid - 16,\n  y: ymid,\n  width: 14,\n  height: 28,\n  latex: true,\n});\n\nfigure.curve((t) => [t ** 2 - 1, t + 1], {\n  domain: [-4, 3],\n  samples: 701,\n  stroke: \"red\",\n  width: 1,\n});\n\nfor (const t of [-2, -1, 0, 1, 2]) {\n  figure.point(t ** 2 - 1, t + 1, {\n    radius: 3,\n    fill: \"red\",\n    stroke: \"red\",\n  });\n}\n\n// Arrow indicating increasing t at the curve's endpoint.\nfigure.polygon()\n  .points(\"3,0 -3,-3 -3,3\")\n  .fill(\"red\")\n  .stroke(\"red\")\n  .transform(\n    `translate(${xmid + 8 * scale},${ymid - 4 * scale}) ` +\n    `rotate(${Math.atan2(-1, 6) * 180 / Math.PI})`\n  );\n\nfor (const [label, dx, dy] of [\n  [\"t=-2\", 3, 0.5],\n  [\"t=-1\", 0.25, -0.5],\n  [\"t=0\", -2.15, -1.2],\n  [\"t=1\", 0.15, -2],\n  [\"t=2\", 3, -3],\n]) {\n  figure.label(label, {\n    x: xmid + dx * scale,\n    y: ymid + dy * scale,\n    width: 60,\n    height: 28,\n    latex: true,\n  });\n}\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xyOt=−2t=−1t=0t=1t=2The parabola defined parametrically by x = t² − 1, y = t + 1, t ∈ ℝ.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"440\" height=\"280\" viewBox=\"0 0 440 280\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"280\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"440,180 433,176 433,184\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"80,0 76,7 84,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"426\" y=\"150\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"88\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"64\" y=\"180\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>O</mi></math></div></foreignObject><polyline points