1072 133.33242326642682,197.7958295812854 133.8560491911428,196.730984797044 134.39840669133605,195.65531578167233 134.95954270824907,194.56951924668732 135.53948460442638,193.47429567756453 136.13824008613332,192.37034873210848 136.7557971437026,191.2583846365016 137.39212400986662,190.1391115795733 138.04716913611858,189.01323910582815 138.72086118712946,187.88147750777642 139.41310905322916,186.74453721811068 140.1238018809485,185.6031282022684 140.85280912159686,184.45795935192655 141.59998059783928,183.30973787996692 142.36514658821682,182.15916871745384 143.14811792954032,181.00695391316145 143.94868613706927,179.85379203618862 144.7666235423747,178.70037758219138 145.60168344876513,177.54740038376616 146.45360030414278,176.3955450255074 147.32208989113838,175.24549026426251 148.2068495343592,174.09790845510108 149.10755832456928,172.95346498350986 150.023877359604,171.8128177043211 150.95545000180957,170.6766163878723 151.9019021517783,169.5455021738934 152.86284253814136,168.4201070336056 153.8378630231601,167.30105324051436 154.82653892384957,166.18895285036382 155.82842934834798,165.08440719071893 156.84307754723466,163.9880063606296 157.87001127948744,162.90032874081965 158.90874319275252,161.82194051483964 159.95877121759233,160.75339520160676 161.01957897535925,159.69523319974988 162.0906361993354,158.64798134416253 163.1713991687629,157.61215247515986 164.26131115538053,156.58824502062046 165.35980288206804,155.57674259148578 166.46629299319235,154.5781135909747 167.58018853623668,153.59281083786 168.7008854542829,152.62127120414175 169.82776908891117,151.6639152674374 170.96021469306703,150.72114697839643 172.09758795344112,149.79335334343395 173.23924552189433,148.88090412306434 174.38453555545723,147.9841515460991 175.53279826442045,147.103430039964 176.68336646802936,146.2390559773697 177.8355661572865,145.39132743956173 178.98871706435997,144.56052399635573 180.14213323809219,143.7469065031505 181.29512362509178,142.95071691509898 182.44699265589423,142.17217811859516 183.59704083566584,141.41149378022763 184.74456533892527,140.66884821332658 185.8888606077508,139.94440626222257 187.02921895294062,139.23831320431344 188.1649311575889,138.55069467002377 189.29528708253895,137.88165658072313 190.41957627317214,137.23128510465438 191.53708856699276,136.5996466309056 192.64711470146423,135.98678776144453 193.74894692155445,135.39273532121788 194.84187958644836,134.81749638629998 195.9252097748847,134.26105833006181 196.99823788857742,133.72338888731227 198.06026825318082,133.20443623635038 199.11060971626387,132.70412909884757 200.14857624175704,132.22237685746717 201.17348750034245,131.75906969110787 202.1846694552583,131.31407872764646 203.18145494299475,130.88725621403566 204.16318424836209,130.47843570359976 205.12920567341723,130.08743226035418 206.07887609974014,129.7140426801599 207.01156154355664,129.358045728509 207.92663770321323,129.01920239472165 208.8234904985121,128.69725616232154 209.7015166014263,128.39193329534078 210.56012395771904,128.1029431402918 211.39873229900212,127.82997844352944 212.2167736447734,127.57271568371279 213.01369279398716,127.33081541906239 213.78894780571602,127.10392264909525 214.54201046847444,126.89166719050756 215.27236675778562,126.69366406686123 215.97951728158037,126.50951391171935 216.66297771303186,126.33880338486215 217.32227921043773,126.1811056012044 217.95696882377408,126.03598057202318 218.56660988755846,125.90297565809406 219.15078239966977,125.78162603432284 219.7090833857862,125.67145516545007 220.24112724911618,125.57197529239521 220.74654610510783,125.48268792879782 221.22499010083948,125.40308436730417 221.67612771880357,125.33264619513876 222.0996460648138,125.27084581849182 222.495251139777,125.2171469952461 222.86266809508697,125.1710053755587 223.20164147141153,125.13186904980667 223.51193542065974,125.09917910339813 223.79333391092993,125.07237017794526 224.0456409142559,125.05087103828895 224.2686805769826,125.03410514486009 224.46229737261973,125.02149123085782 224.62635623703562,125.01244388372015 224.76074268587098,125.00637413035922 224.86536291406742,125.00269002562935 224.94014387742152,125.00079724349395 224.98503335609155,125.00009967035405 225,125\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"red\" stroke-width=\"1\"></polyline></svg><figcaption>An astroid.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"Consider the parametric curve ``𝒞`` with equations\n\n```math\nx=f\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y = g\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{for }a\\le t\\le b\\,,\n```\n\nwhere ``f`` is differentiable and ``g`` is continuous on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``. For the moment, let us also assume that ``f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0`` and ``g\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0`` on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, so ``𝒞`` has no points below the ``x``-axis and is traversed from left to right as ``t`` increases from ``a`` to ``b``.\n\nThe region under ``C`` and above the ``x``-axis has area element given by\n\n```math\n\\mathrm{d} A = y\\,\\mathrm{d} x=g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t\\,,\n```\n\nso its area is\n\n```math\nA=\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t\\,.\n```\n\nSimilar arguments can be given for three other cases:\n\n- if ``f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0`` and ``g\\left(t\\right)\\le 0`` on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, then ``\\displaystyle A=-\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t``,\n\n- if ``f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\le 0`` and ``g\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0`` on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, then ``\\displaystyle A=-\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t``,\n\n- if ``f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\le 0`` and ``g\\left(t\\right)\\le 0`` on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, then ``\\displaystyle A=\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t``,\n\nwhere ``A``  is the (positive) area bounded by ``𝒞``, the ``x``-axis, and the vertical lines ``x=f\\left(a\\right)`` and ``x=f\\left(b\\right)``. Combining these results we can see that\n\n```math\n\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t=A_1-A_2\\,,\n```\n\nwhere ``A_1`` is the area lying vertically between ``𝒞`` and that part of the ``x``-axis consisting of points ``x=f\\left(t\\right)`` such that ``g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0``, and ``A_2`` is a similar area corresponding to points where ``g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)< 0``. This formula is valid for arbitrary continuous ``g`` and differentiable ``f``. In particular, if ``𝒞`` is a non–self-intersecting closed curve, then the area of the region bounded by ``𝒞`` is given by\n\n```math\nA=\\begin{cases}\n\\hphantom{-}\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t&\\textrm{if }𝒞\\textrm{ is traversed clockwise as }t\\textrm{ increases,}\\\\\n-\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t&\\textrm{if }𝒞\\textrm{ is traversed counterclockwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n```\n\n!!! example \"Find the area bounded by the ellipse\"\n\n\t```math\n\tx=a\\cos s\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y=b\\sin s\\quad\\textrm{for } 0\\le s\\le 2\\uppi\\,,\\quad\\textrm{where }a>0, b>0\\,.\n\t```\n\n\tThis ellipse is traversed counterclockwise. The area enclosed is\n\n\t```math\n\t\\begin{aligned}\n\tA&=-\\int_0^{2\\uppi}b\\sin s\\left(-a\\sin s\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} s=\\frac{ab}{2}\\int_0^{2\\uppi}\\left(1-\\cos 2s\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} s\\\\\n\t&=\\left.\\frac{ab}{2}s\\right|_0^{2\\uppi}-\\left.\\frac{ab}{4}\\sin 2s\\right|_0^{2\\uppi}=\\uppi ab\\ \\textrm{square units.}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n\t```\n\n","output":"","result":"Consider the parametric curve ``𝒞`` with equations\n\n```math\nx=f\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y = g\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{for }a\\le t\\le b\\,,\n```\n\nwhere ``f`` is differentiable and ``g`` is continuous on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``. For the moment, let us also assume that ``f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0`` and ``g\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0`` on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, so ``𝒞`` has no points below the ``x``-axis and is traversed from left to right as ``t`` increases from ``a`` to ``b``.\n\nThe region under ``C`` and above the ``x``-axis has area element given by\n\n```math\n\\mathrm{d} A = y\\,\\mathrm{d} x=g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t\\,,\n```\n\nso its area is\n\n```math\nA=\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t\\,.\n```\n\nSimilar arguments can be given for three other cases:\n\n- if ``f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0`` and ``g\\left(t\\right)\\le 0`` on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, then ``\\displaystyle A=-\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t``,\n\n- if ``f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\le 0`` and ``g\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0`` on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, then ``\\displaystyle A=-\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t``,\n\n- if ``f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\le 0`` and ``g\\left(t\\right)\\le 0`` on ``\\left[a,b\\right]``, then ``\\displaystyle A=\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t``,\n\nwhere ``A``  is the (positive) area bounded by ``𝒞``, the ``x``-axis, and the vertical lines ``x=f\\left(a\\right)`` and ``x=f\\left(b\\right)``. Combining these results we can see that\n\n```math\n\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t=A_1-A_2\\,,\n```\n\nwhere ``A_1`` is the area lying vertically between ``𝒞`` and that part of the ``x``-axis consisting of points ``x=f\\left(t\\right)`` such that ``g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0``, and ``A_2`` is a similar area corresponding to points where ``g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)< 0``. This formula is valid for arbitrary continuous ``g`` and differentiable ``f``. In particular, if ``𝒞`` is a non–self-intersecting closed curve, then the area of the region bounded by ``𝒞`` is given by\n\n```math\nA=\\begin{cases}\n\\hphantom{-}\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t&\\textrm{if }𝒞\\textrm{ is traversed clockwise as }t\\textrm{ increases,}\\\\\n-\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t&\\textrm{if }𝒞\\textrm{ is traversed counterclockwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n```\n\n!!! example \"Find the area bounded by the ellipse\"\n\n\t```math\n\tx=a\\cos s\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y=b\\sin s\\quad\\textrm{for } 0\\le s\\le 2\\uppi\\,,\\quad\\textrm{where }a>0, b>0\\,.\n\t```\n\n\tThis ellipse is traversed counterclockwise. The area enclosed is\n\n\t```math\n\t\\begin{aligned}\n\tA&=-\\int_0^{2\\uppi}b\\sin s\\left(-a\\sin s\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} s=\\frac{ab}{2}\\int_0^{2\\uppi}\\left(1-\\cos 2s\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} s\\\\\n\t&=\\left.\\frac{ab}{2}s\\right|_0^{2\\uppi}-\\left.\\frac{ab}{4}\\sin 2s\\right|_0^{2\\uppi}=\\uppi ab\\ \\textrm{square units.}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n\t```\n\n","resultHTML":"<p>Consider the parametric curve <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(𝒞\\)</span> with equations</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nx=f\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y = g\\left(t\\right)\\quad\\textrm{for }a\\le t\\le b\\,,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span> is differentiable and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span> is continuous on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>. For the moment, let us also assume that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0\\)</span> on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(𝒞\\)</span> has no points below the <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span>-axis and is traversed from left to right as <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(t\\)</span> increases from <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(b\\)</span>.</p>\n<p>The region under <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(C\\)</span> and above the <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span>-axis has area element given by</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\mathrm{d} A = y\\,\\mathrm{d} x=g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t\\,,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>so its area is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA=\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>Similar arguments can be given for three other cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(t\\right)\\le 0\\)</span> on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle A=-\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t\\)</span>,</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\le 0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0\\)</span> on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle A=-\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t\\)</span>,</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\le 0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(t\\right)\\le 0\\)</span> on <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left[a,b\\right]\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\displaystyle A=\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t\\)</span>,</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A\\)</span>  is the (positive) area bounded by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(𝒞\\)</span>, the <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span>-axis, and the vertical lines <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=f\\left(a\\right)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=f\\left(b\\right)\\)</span>. Combining these results we can see that</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t=A_1-A_2\\,,\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A_1\\)</span> is the area lying vertically between <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(𝒞\\)</span> and that part of the <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span>-axis consisting of points <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x=f\\left(t\\right)\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\ge 0\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(A_2\\)</span> is a similar area corresponding to points where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)&lt; 0\\)</span>. This formula is valid for arbitrary continuous <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(g\\)</span> and differentiable <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span>. In particular, if <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(𝒞\\)</span> is a non–self-intersecting closed curve, then the area of the region bounded by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(𝒞\\)</span> is given by</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nA=\\begin{cases}\n\\hphantom{-}\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t&amp;\\textrm{if }𝒞\\textrm{ is traversed clockwise as }t\\textrm{ increases,}\\\\\n-\\int_a^b g\\left(t\\right)f^\\prime\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} t&amp;\\textrm{if }𝒞\\textrm{ is traversed counterclockwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n</div>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Find the area bounded by the ellipse</h5>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\nx=a\\cos s\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad y=b\\sin s\\quad\\textrm{for } 0\\le s\\le 2\\uppi\\,,\\quad\\textrm{where }a&gt;0, b&gt;0\\,.\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>This ellipse is traversed counterclockwise. The area enclosed is</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&amp;=-\\int_0^{2\\uppi}b\\sin s\\left(-a\\sin s\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} s=\\frac{ab}{2}\\int_0^{2\\uppi}\\left(1-\\cos 2s\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d} s\\\\\n&amp;=\\left.\\frac{ab}{2}s\\right|_0^{2\\uppi}-\\left.\\frac{ab}{4}\\sin 2s\\right|_0^{2\\uppi}=\\uppi ab\\ \\textrm{square units.}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n</div>\n</aside>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Polar Coordinates and Polar Curves\n\nThe *polar coordinate system* is an alternative to the rectangular (Cartesian) coordinate system for describing the location of points in a plane. Sometimes it is more important to know how far, and in what direction, a point is from the origin than it is to know its Cartesian coordinates. In the polar coordinate system there is an origin (or *pole*), ``O``, and a *polar axis*, a ray (i.e., a half-line) extending from ``O`` horizontally to the right.\n\nThe position of any point ``P`` in the plane is then determined by its polar coordinates ``\\left(r,\\theta\\right)_P``, where\n\n1. ``r`` is the distance from ``O`` to ``P``, and\n\n2. ``\\theta`` is the angle that the ray ``OP`` makes with the polar axis (counterclockwise angles being considered positive).\n\nThe polar axis coincides with the positive ``x``-axis.","output":"","result":"## Polar Coordinates and Polar Curves\n\nThe *polar coordinate system* is an alternative to the rectangular (Cartesian) coordinate system for describing the location of points in a plane. Sometimes it is more important to know how far, and in what direction, a point is from the origin than it is to know its Cartesian coordinates. In the polar coordinate system there is an origin (or *pole*), ``O``, and a *polar axis*, a ray (i.e., a half-line) extending from ``O`` horizontally to the right.\n\nThe position of any point ``P`` in the plane is then determined by its polar coordinates ``\\left(r,\\theta\\right)_P``, where\n\n1. ``r`` is the distance from ``O`` to ``P``, and\n\n2. ``\\theta`` is the angle that the ray ``OP`` makes with the polar axis (counterclockwise angles being considered positive).\n\nThe polar axis coincides with the positive ``x``-axis.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-ES111_071Lecture07-c3906-polar-coordinates-and-polar-curves\">\n<h1>Polar Coordinates and Polar Curves</h1>\n<p>The <em>polar coordinate system</em> is an alternative to the rectangular (Cartesian) coordinate system for describing the location of points in a plane. Sometimes it is more important to know how far, and in what direction, a point is from the origin than it is to know its Cartesian coordinates. In the polar coordinate system there is an origin (or <em>pole</em>), <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(O\\)</span>, and a <em>polar axis</em>, a ray (i.e., a half-line) extending from <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(O\\)</span> horizontally to the right.</p>\n<p>The position of any point <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P\\)</span> in the plane is then determined by its polar coordinates <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(r,\\theta\\right)_P\\)</span>, where</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\)</span> is the distance from <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(O\\)</span> to <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(P\\)</span>, and</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\theta\\)</span> is the angle that the ray <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(OP\\)</span> makes with the polar axis (counterclockwise angles being considered positive).</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The polar axis coincides with the positive <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span>-axis.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 30;\nconst xmid = 5 * scale;\nconst ymid = 5 * scale;\nconst radius = 4 * scale;\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n  width: 10 * scale,\n  height: 10 * scale,\n  caption: \"Polar coordinates.\",\n});\n\nfor (const i of [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11]) {\n  const angle = Math.PI * i / 12;\n  const dx = radius * Math.cos(angle);\n  const dy = radius * Math.sin(angle);\n  figure.line()\n    .x1(xmid + dx).y1(ymid - dy)\n    .x2(xmid - dx).y2(ymid + dy)\n    .stroke(\"black\").strokeWidth(0.5);\n}\n\nfor (let i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {\n  figure.circle()\n    .cx(xmid).cy(ymid).r(i * scale)\n    .stroke(\"black\").fill(\"none\").strokeWidth(0.5);\n}\n\nfor (const [angle, label, dx, dy] of [\n  [Math.PI / 12, String.raw`\\frac{\\pi}{12}`, 5, -10],\n  [Math.PI / 6, String.raw`\\frac{\\pi}{6}`, 4, -12],\n  [Math.PI / 4, String.raw`\\frac{\\pi}{4}`, 2, -14],\n  [Math.PI / 3, String.raw`\\frac{\\pi}{3}`, 0, -18],\n  [5 * Math.PI / 12, String.raw`\\frac{5\\pi}{12}`, -2, -22],\n]) {\n  figure.label(label, {\n    x: xmid + radius * Math.cos(angle) + dx,\n    y: ymid - radius * Math.sin(angle) + dy,\n    width: 28,\n    height: 36,\n    latex: true,\n  });\n}\n\nfigure.axes({\n  origin: [xmid, ymid],\n  scale,\n  x: {\n    ticks: [1, 2, 3, 4].map((value) => ({\n      value,\n      label: String.raw`\\quad ${value}`,\n      length: 3,\n    })),\n  },\n});\n\nfigure.label(\"O\", {\n  x: xmid - 16, y: ymid, width: 14, height: 28, latex: true,\n});\n\nconst px = xmid + 3 * scale * Math.cos(Math.PI / 6);\nconst py = ymid - 3 * scale * Math.sin(Math.PI / 6);\n\nfigure.circle().cx(px).cy(py).r(3).stroke(\"red\").fill(\"red\");\nfigure.label(String.raw`\\left(3,\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)_P`, {\n  x: px - 20,\n  y: py - 25,\n  width: 70,\n  height: 36,\n  latex: true,\n});\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"π12π6π4π35π12xy1234O(3,π6)PPolar coordinates.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"300\" height=\"300\" viewBox=\"0 0 300 300\"><line x1=\"265.9110991546882\" y1=\"118.9417145876975\" x2=\"34.0889008453118\" y2=\"181.0582854123025\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"253.92304845413264\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"46.07695154586736\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"234.8528137423857\" y1=\"65.14718625761431\" x2=\"65.1471862576143\" y2=\"234.8528137423857\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"46.076951545867374\" x2=\"89.99999999999999\" y2=\"253.92304845413264\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"181.0582854123025\" y1=\"34.0889008453118\" x2=\"118.9417145876975\" y2=\"265.9110991546882\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"118.94171458769753\" y1=\"34.0889008453118\" x2=\"181.05828541230247\" y2=\"265.9110991546882\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"90.00000000000003\" y1=\"46.07695154586736\" x2=\"209.99999999999997\" y2=\"253.92304845413264\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"65.14718625761431\" y1=\"65.1471862576143\" x2=\"234.8528137423857\" y2=\"234.8528137423857\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"46.07695154586736\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"253.92304845413264\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"34.08890084531181\" y1=\"118.94171458769748\" x2=\"265.9110991546882\" y2=\"181.05828541230252\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"150\" r=\"30\" stroke=\"black\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></circle><circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"150\" r=\"60\" stroke=\"black\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></circle><circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"150\" r=\"90\" stroke=\"black\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></circle><circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"150\" r=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></circle><foreignObject x=\"270.9110991546882\" y=\"108.9417145876975\" width=\"28\" height=\"36\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>12</mn></mfrac></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"257.92304845413264\" y=\"78\" width=\"28\" height=\"36\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"236.8528137423857\" y=\"51.14718625761431\" width=\"28\" height=\"36\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"210\" y=\"28.076951545867374\" width=\"28\" height=\"36\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"179.0582854123025\" y=\"12.088900845311798\" width=\"28\" height=\"36\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>12</mn></mfrac></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"300\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"300,150 293,146 293,154\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"150,0 146,7 154,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"286\" y=\"120\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"158\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"154\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"159\" y=\"158\" width=\"42\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" style=\"display: flex; width: 100%; height: 100%; justify-content: center; align-items: flex-start;\"><math><mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"154\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"189\" y=\"158\" width=\"42\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" style=\"display: flex; width: 100%; height: 100%; justify-content: center; align-items: flex-start;\"><math><mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"154\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"219\" y=\"158\" width=\"42\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" style=\"display: flex; width: 100%; height: 100%; justify-content: center; align-items: flex-start;\"><math><mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mn>3</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"270\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"154\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"249\" y=\"158\" width=\"42\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\" style=\"display: flex; width: 100%; height: 100%; justify-content: center; align-items: flex-start;\"><math><mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mn>4</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"134\" y=\"150\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>O</mi></math></div></foreignObject><circle cx=\"227.94228634059948\" cy=\"105\" r=\"3\" stroke=\"red\" fill=\"red\"></circle><foreignObject x=\"207.94228634059948\" y=\"80\" width=\"70\" height=\"36\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><msub><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption>Polar coordinates.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"Unlike rectangular coordinates, the polar coordinates of a point are not unique. The polar coordinates ``\\left(r,\\theta_1\\right)_P`` and ``\\left(r,\\theta_2\\right)_P`` represent the same point provided ``\\theta_1`` and ``\\theta_2`` differ by an integer multiple of ``2\\uppi``. For instance, the polar coordinates\n\n```math\n\\left(3,\\frac{\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P=\\left(3,\\frac{13\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P=\\left(3,\\frac{-11\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P=\\left(-3,\\frac{7\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P=\\left(-3,\\frac{-5\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P\n```\n\nall represent the same point with Cartesian coordinates ``\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt 3}{2}, \\frac{3}{2}\\right)``. In addition, the origin ``O`` has polar coordinates ``\\left(0,\\theta\\right)_P`` for any value of ``\\theta``. (If we go zero distance from ``O``, it doesn’t matter in what direction we go.)\n\nIf we want to consider both rectangular and polar coordinate systems in the same plane, and we choose the positive ``x``-axis as the polar axis, then the relationships between the rectangular coordinates of a point and its polar coordinates are\n\n```math\n\\begin{cases}\nx = r\\cos\\theta\\\\\ny = r\\sin\\theta\n\\end{cases}\n\\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad\n\\begin{cases}\nr^2 = x^2+y^2\\\\\n\\tan\\theta = \\frac{y}{x}\n\\end{cases}\n```\n\nA single equation in ``x`` and ``y`` generally represents a curve in the plane with respect to the Cartesian coordinate system. Similarly, a single equation in ``r`` and ``\\theta`` generally represents a curve with respect to the polar coordinate system. The conversion formulas above can be used to convert one representation of a curve into the other.\n\n!!! example \"Find the Cartesian equation of the curve represented by the polar equation ``r=2a\\cos\\theta``; hence, identify the curve.\"\n\n\tThe polar equation can be transformed to Cartesian coordinates if we first multiply it by ``r``:\n\n\t```math\n\t\\begin{aligned}\n\tr^2&=2ar\\cos\\theta\\\\\n\tx^2+y^2&=2ax\\\\\n\t\\left(x-a\\right)^2+y^2&=a^2\n\t\\end{aligned}\n\t```\n\n\tThe given polar equation ``r=2a\\cos\\theta`` thus represents a circle with centre ``\\left(a, 0\\right)`` and radius ``a``.\n\nThe graph of an equation of the form ``r=f\\left(\\theta\\right)`` is called the polar graph of the function ``f``. Some polar graphs can be recognized easily if the polar equation is transformed to rectangular form. For others, this transformation does not help; the rectangular equation may be too complicated to be recognizable. In these cases one must resort to constructing a table of values and plotting points.\n\n!!! example \"Sketch the polar curve ``r=a\\left(1-\\cos\\theta\\right)``, where ``a>0``.\"\n\n\tTransformation to rectangular coordinates is not much help here; the resulting equation is ``\\left(x^2+y^2+ax\\right)^2=a^2\\left(x^2+y^2\\right)`` (verify this), which we do not recognize. Therefore, we will make a plot.\n\t\n\tBecause it is shaped like a heart, this curve is called a cardioid.","output":"","result":"Unlike rectangular coordinates, the polar coordinates of a point are not unique. The polar coordinates ``\\left(r,\\theta_1\\right)_P`` and ``\\left(r,\\theta_2\\right)_P`` represent the same point provided ``\\theta_1`` and ``\\theta_2`` differ by an integer multiple of ``2\\uppi``. For instance, the polar coordinates\n\n```math\n\\left(3,\\frac{\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P=\\left(3,\\frac{13\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P=\\left(3,\\frac{-11\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P=\\left(-3,\\frac{7\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P=\\left(-3,\\frac{-5\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P\n```\n\nall represent the same point with Cartesian coordinates ``\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt 3}{2}, \\frac{3}{2}\\right)``. In addition, the origin ``O`` has polar coordinates ``\\left(0,\\theta\\right)_P`` for any value of ``\\theta``. (If we go zero distance from ``O``, it doesn’t matter in what direction we go.)\n\nIf we want to consider both rectangular and polar coordinate systems in the same plane, and we choose the positive ``x``-axis as the polar axis, then the relationships between the rectangular coordinates of a point and its polar coordinates are\n\n```math\n\\begin{cases}\nx = r\\cos\\theta\\\\\ny = r\\sin\\theta\n\\end{cases}\n\\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad\n\\begin{cases}\nr^2 = x^2+y^2\\\\\n\\tan\\theta = \\frac{y}{x}\n\\end{cases}\n```\n\nA single equation in ``x`` and ``y`` generally represents a curve in the plane with respect to the Cartesian coordinate system. Similarly, a single equation in ``r`` and ``\\theta`` generally represents a curve with respect to the polar coordinate system. The conversion formulas above can be used to convert one representation of a curve into the other.\n\n!!! example \"Find the Cartesian equation of the curve represented by the polar equation ``r=2a\\cos\\theta``; hence, identify the curve.\"\n\n\tThe polar equation can be transformed to Cartesian coordinates if we first multiply it by ``r``:\n\n\t```math\n\t\\begin{aligned}\n\tr^2&=2ar\\cos\\theta\\\\\n\tx^2+y^2&=2ax\\\\\n\t\\left(x-a\\right)^2+y^2&=a^2\n\t\\end{aligned}\n\t```\n\n\tThe given polar equation ``r=2a\\cos\\theta`` thus represents a circle with centre ``\\left(a, 0\\right)`` and radius ``a``.\n\nThe graph of an equation of the form ``r=f\\left(\\theta\\right)`` is called the polar graph of the function ``f``. Some polar graphs can be recognized easily if the polar equation is transformed to rectangular form. For others, this transformation does not help; the rectangular equation may be too complicated to be recognizable. In these cases one must resort to constructing a table of values and plotting points.\n\n!!! example \"Sketch the polar curve ``r=a\\left(1-\\cos\\theta\\right)``, where ``a>0``.\"\n\n\tTransformation to rectangular coordinates is not much help here; the resulting equation is ``\\left(x^2+y^2+ax\\right)^2=a^2\\left(x^2+y^2\\right)`` (verify this), which we do not recognize. Therefore, we will make a plot.\n\t\n\tBecause it is shaped like a heart, this curve is called a cardioid.","resultHTML":"<p>Unlike rectangular coordinates, the polar coordinates of a point are not unique. The polar coordinates <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(r,\\theta_1\\right)_P\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(r,\\theta_2\\right)_P\\)</span> represent the same point provided <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\theta_1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\theta_2\\)</span> differ by an integer multiple of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(2\\uppi\\)</span>. For instance, the polar coordinates</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\left(3,\\frac{\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P=\\left(3,\\frac{13\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P=\\left(3,\\frac{-11\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P=\\left(-3,\\frac{7\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P=\\left(-3,\\frac{-5\\uppi}{6}\\right)_P\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>all represent the same point with Cartesian coordinates <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt 3}{2}, \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\)</span>. In addition, the origin <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(O\\)</span> has polar coordinates <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(0,\\theta\\right)_P\\)</span> for any value of <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\theta\\)</span>. (If we go zero distance from <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(O\\)</span>, it doesn’t matter in what direction we go.)</p>\n<p>If we want to consider both rectangular and polar coordinate systems in the same plane, and we choose the positive <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span>-axis as the polar axis, then the relationships between the rectangular coordinates of a point and its polar coordinates are</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx = r\\cos\\theta\\\\\ny = r\\sin\\theta\n\\end{cases}\n\\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad\n\\begin{cases}\nr^2 = x^2+y^2\\\\\n\\tan\\theta = \\frac{y}{x}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>A single equation in <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(x\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(y\\)</span> generally represents a curve in the plane with respect to the Cartesian coordinate system. Similarly, a single equation in <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\)</span> and <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\theta\\)</span> generally represents a curve with respect to the polar coordinate system. The conversion formulas above can be used to convert one representation of a curve into the other.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Find the Cartesian equation of the curve represented by the polar equation <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r=2a\\cos\\theta\\)</span>; hence, identify the curve.</h5>\n<p>The polar equation can be transformed to Cartesian coordinates if we first multiply it by <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r\\)</span>:</p>\n<div data-type=\"equation\" class=\"math-tex\">\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^2&amp;=2ar\\cos\\theta\\\\\nx^2+y^2&amp;=2ax\\\\\n\\left(x-a\\right)^2+y^2&amp;=a^2\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n</div>\n<p>The given polar equation <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r=2a\\cos\\theta\\)</span> thus represents a circle with centre <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(a, 0\\right)\\)</span> and radius <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a\\)</span>.</p>\n</aside>\n<p>The graph of an equation of the form <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r=f\\left(\\theta\\right)\\)</span> is called the polar graph of the function <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(f\\)</span>. Some polar graphs can be recognized easily if the polar equation is transformed to rectangular form. For others, this transformation does not help; the rectangular equation may be too complicated to be recognizable. In these cases one must resort to constructing a table of values and plotting points.</p>\n<aside data-type=\"example\">\n<h5>Sketch the polar curve <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(r=a\\left(1-\\cos\\theta\\right)\\)</span>, where <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(a&gt;0\\)</span>.</h5>\n<p>Transformation to rectangular coordinates is not much help here; the resulting equation is <span class=\"math-tex\" data-type=\"tex\">\\(\\left(x^2+y^2+ax\\right)^2=a^2\\left(x^2+y^2\\right)\\)</span> (verify this), which we do not recognize. Therefore, we will make a plot.</p>\n<p>Because it is shaped like a heart, this curve is called a cardioid.</p>\n</aside>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 20;\nconst xmid = 6 * scale;\nconst ymid = 6 * scale;\nconst radius = 5 * scale;\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n  width: 12 * scale,\n  height: 12 * scale,\n  caption: \"A cardioid.\",\n});\n\nfor (const i of [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11]) {\n  const angle = Math.PI * i / 12;\n  const dx = radius * Math.cos(angle);\n  const dy = radius * Math.sin(angle);\n  figure.line()\n    .x1(xmid + dx).y1(ymid - dy)\n    .x2(xmid - dx).y2(ymid + dy)\n    .stroke(\"black\").strokeWidth(0.5);\n}\n\nfor (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n  figure.circle()\n    .cx(xmid).cy(ymid).r(i * scale)\n    .stroke(\"black\").fill(\"none\").strokeWidth(0.5);\n}\n\nfigure.axes({\n  origin: [xmid, ymid],\n  scale,\n  x: { ticks: [{ value: 2, label: \"a\" }] },\n});\n\nfigure.label(\"O\", {\n  x: xmid - 16, y: ymid, width: 14, height: 28, latex: true,\n});\n\nfigure.curve((theta) => {\n  const r = 2 * (1 - Math.cos(theta));\n  return [r * Math.cos(theta), r * Math.sin(theta)];\n}, {\n  domain: [0, 2 * Math.PI],\n  samples: 630,\n  stroke: \"red\",\n  width: 1,\n});\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xyaOA cardioid.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"240\" height=\"240\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 240\"><line x1=\"216.59258262890683\" y1=\"94.11809548974793\" x2=\"23.407417371093175\" y2=\"145.88190451025207\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"206.60254037844388\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"33.39745962155612\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"190.71067811865476\" y1=\"49.28932188134526\" x2=\"49.289321881345245\" y2=\"190.71067811865476\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"170\" y1=\"33.39745962155614\" x2=\"69.99999999999999\" y2=\"206.60254037844385\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"145.88190451025207\" y1=\"23.407417371093175\" x2=\"94.11809548974793\" y2=\"216.59258262890683\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"94.11809548974793\" y1=\"23.407417371093175\" x2=\"145.88190451025207\" y2=\"216.59258262890683\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"70.00000000000003\" y1=\"33.39745962155612\" x2=\"169.99999999999997\" y2=\"206.60254037844388\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"49.28932188134526\" y1=\"49.289321881345245\" x2=\"190.71067811865476\" y2=\"190.71067811865476\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"33.39745962155612\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"206.60254037844388\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><line x1=\"23.407417371093175\" y1=\"94.1180954897479\" x2=\"216.59258262890683\" y2=\"145.8819045102521\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></line><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"120\" r=\"20\" stroke=\"black\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></circle><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"120\" r=\"40\" stroke=\"black\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></circle><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"120\" r=\"60\" stroke=\"black\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></circle><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"120\" r=\"80\" stroke=\"black\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></circle><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"120\" r=\"100\" stroke=\"black\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"0.5\"></circle><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"240,120 233,116 233,124\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"120,0 116,7 124,